WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:57.039 --> 00:01:01.520
Let's start with the basics. COP stands

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.680
for the Conference of the Parties. It's

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.680
the annual meeting for the almost 200

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.680
countries signed up to the UN climate

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.880
treaty. This year it's happening in the

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.640
Scottish city of Glasgow. 25,000 people

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.040
are expected—world leaders with all

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.400
their staff as well as NGOs, activists,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.799
private companies—

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.640
all things climate, basically. These

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:25.560
meetings have been happening for almost

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.560
30 years and it's been a rough ride.

00:01:55.439 --> 00:01:59.530
But in 2015 there was finally a

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:02.000
breakthrough ...

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.280
... with a legally binding international

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.119
treaty known as the Paris Agreement.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.599
For the first time almost all countries

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:13.760
in the world agreed on a clear goal: they

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:16.239
would limit the rise in global warming

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.640
to "well below" 2 degrees while still

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:21.040
"aiming for" 1.5 degrees compared to

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.440
pre-industrial levels. And to get there

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.599
they agreed we need net zero emissions

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.520
globally by the second half of this

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:29.920
century. That's where the amount of

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.680
greenhouse gases emitted is the same as

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.959
the amount that we remove from our

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:38.400
atmosphere. 

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.440
So in Paris they

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.840
agreed on the objectives, which was hard

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.720
enough. But how do we actually achieve

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.560
them?

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.640
Well, it was decided that individual

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.959
countries should come up with their own

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.239
climate action plans — what are called

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:01.360
nationally determined contributions—NDCs

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:02.720
for short.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.640
They might include anything from

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.640
switching to electric vehicles to

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.280
boosting renewable energy sources and

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.920
protecting forests. 

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.560
The problem is that, taken

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:26.000
together, that first round of national

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.920
pledges falls way short. By 2030 they

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.920
would lead to a cut in global emissions

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.879
of less than 1% compared with

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.680
2010 levels—nowhere near what's needed

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:37.440
to keep us on track for 1.5 degrees.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.760
What's needed, according to the world's

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.159
top climate scientists, is more than a

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.200
7% cut every year from now

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:46.640
until 2030. To put that into perspective,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.280
last year when the world locked down

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.519
because of COVID-19, global emissions

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.879
dropped by just over 6%.

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:56.959
So, imagine, we need to do that and more

00:03:54.879 --> 00:03:57.920
every single year until the end of the

00:03:56.959 --> 00:03:59.840
decade.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.080
Now, luckily the Paris

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.920
Agreement also says that every five

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.000
years countries have to come back with

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:21.120
more ambitious pledges. It's back to

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.440
those NDCs again. And unless they really

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.360
are ambitious enough this year, it'll

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.479
probably be too late.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:31.280
This round of NDCs is really the last

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:32.320
chance to keep the 1.5 degree target in

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.600
reach

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:38.000
and that's what makes COP26 so important.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.639
That means

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.479
everyone's got to get in on the act,

00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:58.400
especially the biggest emitters.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.840
The US has

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.360
already made a more ambitious

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.400
pledge, but there's still a question mark

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.400
about China, the world's biggest emitter.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.560
It has made some big announcements, like

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:21.680
promising to reach net zero emissions by

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.720
2060 and no longer funding coal projects

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.320
abroad.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.120
But it doesn't look like President Xi

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.120
Jinping is even going to Glasgow.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.759
And when it comes to hitting that 2030

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:34.240
deadline to stay on track for 1.5

00:05:31.759 --> 00:05:36.560
degrees, the world just can't do it

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.479
without China. 

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.280
So, the new NDCs, who's

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:47.919
pledging what and who can be persuaded

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.759
to pledge more—that's all going to be a

00:05:47.919 --> 00:05:52.400
big issue in Glasgow.

00:05:49.759 --> 00:05:55.199
But another big issue is money, or the

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:57.199
lack of it. Back in 2009, developed

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:59.360
countries promised $100 billion

00:05:57.199 --> 00:06:01.759
a year by 2020 to help

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.840
developing nations fight climate change.

00:06:01.759 --> 00:06:06.319
But that deadline's now been and gone

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:09.360
and there's still $20 billion short. 

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:23.759
And that issue of

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.600
adaptation, that's another big item on

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:28.000
the save the planet wish list that all

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.080
those COP delegates need to get through.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.720
It means things like improving early

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:35.280
warning systems for storms and floods.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.680
Also on the list: how to create a global

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.520
carbon market that actually works, how to

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.400
protect the environment so that nature

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:44.800
can do its part in absorbing more carbon,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.680
how to reduce methane, another greenhouse gas,

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:52.160
and how to completely phase out coal.

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:54.960
Here's the thing: this is also the first

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:56.479
COP where the science is unequivocal.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.280
Hanging over all those leaders in

00:06:56.479 --> 00:07:01.520
Glasgow is a recent landmark report from

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.520
the world's top climate scientists

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.599
setting out the disasters in store for

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.520
us if we don't act now.

00:07:05.599 --> 00:07:11.560
Just look at the last few months to get

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.560
a sense of where things are going.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:05.039
However hard COP negotiations are, in

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.639
some ways, they're also the easy part.

00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:09.120
Making sure that countries actually

00:08:06.639 --> 00:08:11.199
deliver on the pledges and the plans is

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.680
an even bigger challenge.

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:15.640
But the stakes are way too high to have

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.960
our leaders cop out now.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:20.960
If there's another story or topic you

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.720
want to get your head around, let us know.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.039
In the meantime check out our recent

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:26.720
episode on space junk and make sure to

00:08:25.039 --> 00:08:30.680
subscribe to our YouTube channel so you

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.680
don't miss us next week.

